    "comparison_vs_historical_et2_delta_pct": -0.186,
    "root_cause_1_crack_tip_resolution": "Method B Native has a 2x finer crack-tip resolution (h_tip ~ 1.91 um, h/l0 ~ 0.25) than the Canonical Reference (h = 3.75 um, h/l0 = 0.50) and Historical ET2 (h ~ 2.85 um, h/l0 ~ 0.38). In phase-field fracture mechanics, finer crack-tip resolution captures the singular stress concentration more sharply, yielding a slightly higher discrete strain energy density in the tip element that triggers crack initiation at a slightly lower global load (-1.947%). Historical ET2 matched the reference peak within -0.186% because its crack-tip mesh size (2.85 um) closely matched the reference (3.75 um).",
    "root_cause_2_pre_analysis_state_window": "Method B Native was generated from a Step-1 pre-analysis (u in [0, 0.0050] mm, fully pre-peak elastic regime). At u = 0.0050 mm, the crack had not propagated, so the error indicator refined a wide zone around the notch tip but left the far ligament (x > 0.75 mm) coarse (h ~ 19.7 um). In contrast, Historical ET2 was generated from Step-2 pre-analysis (u in [0.0050, 0.0100] mm, fully localized post-peak regime), concentrating refinement along a narrower corridor that traversed the entire ligament (h <= 9.8 um).",
    "root_cause_3_energy_and_work_accumulation": "Because Method B Native transitions to a coarser mesh (h ~ 19.7 um > 2.6 l0) on the far ligament, the diffuse phase-field zone cannot localize as sharply as on h <= l0/2 meshes. This mesh-dependent regularized softening tail maintains higher reaction forces across u in [0.0060, 0.0075] mm (F ~ 0.50 - 0.60 kN vs ~0.00 kN in Ref), delaying snap-through to u = 0.0080 mm and dissipating more cumulative external work (W_ext = 3.332 mJ vs 2.828 mJ in ET2 and 2.359 mJ in Ref) and UEL fracture energy (E_frac = 3.032 mJ vs 2.539 mJ in ET2 and 2.340 mJ in Ref)."
  },
